WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:13.840
Here we have a hemoglobin structure with the PDB&nbsp;
ID 2hbs. This structure is a mutant which is known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.960
to cause sickle cell disease. Upon examining&nbsp;
the interface between the two globular regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.280
it appears that certain residues have their side&nbsp;
chains shown. To select one of the atoms in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:35.440
side chains, use Control plus left click.&nbsp;
To broaden the scope of the selection, use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.760
the Up Arrow, and to narrow the scope,&nbsp;
use the Down Arrow. You can use the Right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.560
Arrow to invert the selection and the Left&nbsp;
Arrow to undo the last selection action.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:57.840
To select multiple atoms at once,&nbsp;
use Control, Shift, and left click.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.080
To select a broader region&nbsp;
of the protein, keep the&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.240
Control button pressed down and use a&nbsp;
left mouse drag to reveal a rectangle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.320
Upon releasing the mouse, everything&nbsp;
within the rectangle becomes selected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:25.840
Pressing the Control button and clicking outside&nbsp;
of the structure deselects the selected region.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.760
Chimera's command line is readily&nbsp;
accessible under the Favorites menu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.120
Now we can use the command line to&nbsp;
perform selections within the structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.160
Unsurprisingly, the key word for these commands&nbsp;
is "select". Let's say you wanted to select a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.440
particular range of residue positions within&nbsp;
the structure. To do this, you would type into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:58.560
the command line: select, colon, and the desired&nbsp;
residue position range. Upon hitting Enter, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.840
positions are highlighted in green. If you wanted&nbsp;
to limit the selection to a particular chain, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.960
would type a period followed by the desired chain&nbsp;
code. Now the selection only applies to chain A.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.000
To select a specific residue within a&nbsp;
chain, the format is the same as before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:27.600
except the residue range is replaced&nbsp;
with a single position. To select the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:35.840
same residue position across a number of&nbsp;
chains, simply add a chain range as shown.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.400
Research has shown that the most common&nbsp;
type of sickle cell disease-causing mutation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.320
is a mutation from glutamic acid to valine&nbsp;
at position 6 of the hemoglobin beta chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:02.560
chain H. To locate this position, we can&nbsp;
select by Sequence. Upon selecting chain H,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.640
we are greeted with the entire amino acid&nbsp;
sequence of that chain. Hovering over each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.960
of these letters reveals the position ID&nbsp;
label at the bottom of the window. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:22.080
way we eventually come to valine 6. To select&nbsp;
this residue, click and drag over its letter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:30.720
Notice the residue highlighted in the graphics&nbsp;
window. Now under Actions, Atoms and Bonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.560
we can show the side chain; and&nbsp;
then we can focus in on the residue.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.040
Once you have obtained a clear view of&nbsp;
the residue, you may want to label it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:51.760
To do this, go to Actions, Label,&nbsp;
Residue, and choose Name + Specifier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:59.520
This label provides the three letter code of&nbsp;
the amino acid, its number, and its chain. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:07.440
can adjust label parameters such as font size by&nbsp;
again going to Actions, Label and Options. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.840
label comes in small, so it's a good idea&nbsp;
to set the font size to something larger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.400
If you would like the label to contrast with&nbsp;
the surrounding protein, you can go to Actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:27.040
Color, and under All Options, selectively&nbsp;
apply the coloring to Residue Labels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:35.520
Then you can choose a color&nbsp;
that provides better contrast.

